# Normalise every text file to LF in the repository, whatever the contributor's
# platform. Working copies still check out in the platform's native form -- this
# governs what is STORED, not what you edit.
#
# This must come FIRST: in .gitattributes the LAST matching pattern wins, so the two
# specific rules below deliberately override it. Moving this line to the end would
# silently revoke both.
#
# It is pinned here rather than left to `core.autocrlf` because that setting is a
# property of each CLONE, not of the project. Every file in this tree is already
# stored LF (verified with `git ls-files --eol`: one deliberate exception, below), so
# this rule renormalises nothing today -- it exists so that a contributor whose clone
# is configured differently cannot commit CRLF without noticing. That failure mode is
# quiet and expensive: it lands as a whole-file diff that reviews as "everything
# changed", hiding the real change inside it.
* text=auto

# Preserve CRLF verbatim in the line-endings test fixture (MANUAL-TEST §4.2 round-trip).
# `-text` disables all normalisation, so the CRLFs survive the rule above.
tests/fixtures/crlf-doc.md -text

# Windows batch files must check out with CRLF wherever they are cloned: cmd.exe
# reads a .bat by byte offset as it executes, so an LF-only file can mis-seek on
# `goto`/`call` and run the wrong branch. Nothing warns; it simply does the wrong
# thing. Git stores LF and converts on checkout.
*.bat text eol=crlf
